{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9d46dd9c1e285975", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t--- PAGE 4 ---\n2\t1. Visual Branch:To capture purely visual details without auditory bias, we feed the mute video\n3\tstream (60s) into the model. This isolation forces the model to rely solely on visual cues, generating\n4\ta comprehensive description free from auditory interference.\n5\t2. Hierarchical Audio Branch:Overlapping speech and background noise severely degrade ASR\n6\tand captioning accuracy in standard pipelines. To address this, we first robustly decouple the\n7\tsoundtrack into vocal and background tracks using Demucs [37].\n8\t•Vocal Caption:The clean vocal stream is then processed by Qwen3-Omni-Thinking for\n9\thigh-fidelity ASR, after which the isolated audio, video frames, and transcripts are jointly\n10\tused to attribute speaker characteristics such as tone, emotion, and gender, yielding a\n11\tstructured V ocal Caption.\n12\t•BGM Captions:In parallel, to capture temporal dynamics often missed by global sum-\n13\tmaries, we segment the non-vocal background track into 15-second windows and generate\n14\tindependent BGM Captions, thereby modeling rapid acoustic transitions and environmental\n15\tshifts at a higher temporal resolution.\n16\tAudio-Visual Joint Reasoning & Temporal Integration.To mitigate modality dominance common\n17\tin end-to-end generation, we employQwen3-Omni-Thinkingfor joint reasoning. By conditioning on\n18\trich unimodal priors (Visual, ASR, V ocal, and BGM captions) alongside raw audiovisual data, the\n19\tmodel generates a Joint Caption that explicitly aligns visual events with auditory cues. Additionally,\n20\tfor videos exceeding 60s, a Temporal Integration step synthesizes segment-level outputs into a\n21\tcoherent narrative.\n22\tRating-based Filtering.Publicly sourced data inevitably contains noise. To ensure reliability, we\n23\timplement a rigorous quality control mechanism. The generated joint captions are evaluated by\n24\tQwen3-Omni-Thinkingacross three dimensions: Visual Accuracy, Audio Fidelity, and Alignment\n25\tPrecision. Only samples achieving high confidence scores are retained. To quantify the residual\n26\tnoise after this automatic filtering stage, we further conduct a manual verification on 1,000 randomly\n27\tsampled training examples in Appendix B.3.\n28\tData Curation & Diversity.We curate a composite source pool integrating established benchmarks\n29\t(including A VE [ 38], VGG-Sound [ 39], Condensed Movies [ 40], A VQA [ 41], Trailer30K [ 42], and\n30\tMPII-MV AD [ 43]) and footage from YouTube. Crucially, prior to processing, we employQwen3-\n31\tOmni-Instructto conduct quality filtering and semantic classification on these source videos. This\n32\tensures high audio-visual richness in dataset source video clips. As shown in Figure 2 and 3, this\n33\tcuration strategy yields a wide distribution across semantic categories and video durations.\n34\tFigure 2:Distribution of Video Categories in\n35\tA VCap-100K.\n36\tFigure 3:Video Duration Distribution of\n37\tA VCap-100K.\n38\t3.2 Detail-Aware-GRPO (Da-GRPO)\n39\tDa-GRPO Reward Formulation.Inspired by Chai et al. [44], to quantify informational density,\n40\twe utilize a fact-checking mechanism anchored in the ground truth. During the dataset construction\n41\tphase, the Judge Model Mjudgefirst raises Ngranular questions based on the ground-truth caption\n42\tCgt. These questions are explicitly categorized into three distinct subsets: Visual (Q v), Audio (Q a),\n43\tand Audio-Visual Joint interactions ( Qav). Subsequently, the Judge Model answers these questions\n44\tusing Cgtas the oracle context, yielding canonical answers agt\n45\tk. This results in a predefined probe set\n46\tQ=Q v∪ Qa∪ Qav, comprising the collection of all question-answer pairs{(q k, agt\n47\tk)}N\n48\tk=1.\n49\t4\n50\t--- PAGE 5 ---\n51\tGround Truth\n52\t...with  fans  pressing  against  barriers  in the \n53\tbackground ; the speaker’s  volume  rises  sharply  \n54\tas he says  \"If he doesn't  want  to come  back,  he \n55\tcan  get off, both  of them\"  in an aggressive,  \n56\tenunciated  tone  with  a British  accent , while  \n57\tthe score  swells  with  warm  French  horn  \n58\tundertones  and  staccato  string  patterns,  \n59\tshifting  to an energetic,  communal  mood  that  \n60\taligns  with  the crowd’s  enthusiasm ...1.Raise atomic questions\n61\tWhat accent was the speaker using \n62\twhen he was aggressive?\n63\t2.Answer with given captions\n64\t3.Score Predicted AnswerBritish BritishPredicted Caption\n65\t...while  plenty  of fans  were  crowding  and  \n66\tpressing  against  the barriers ; the speaker’s  \n67\tvoice  booms  as he snaps  aggressively,  'If he \n68\tdoesn't  want  to come  back,  he can  get off, \n69\tboth  of them,'  in a clear,  fierce  British  accent , \n70\tjoined  by a swelling  score  of warm  French  \n71\thorns  and  staccato  strings  that  shifts  into  a \n72\tvibrant,  shared  energy  mirroring  the crowd’s  \n73\tpassion ...\n74\t4.Reward*NFigure 4:Overview of the Da-GRPO Reward calculation.The workflow follows aRaise-Answer-\n75\tCheckparadigm: (1)Raise: A set of fine-grained audio-visual probes is retrieved from the dataset;\n76\t(2)Answer: The generated caption acts as the sole context for a Judge Model to answer these probes;\n77\t(3)Score: The predicted answers are semantically verified against ground-truth answers to compute\n78\ta dense, atomic-level reward.\n79\tFor a candidate generated caption ˆCi, the reward computation proceeds through a three-stage process,\n80\tas illustrated in Figure 4:\n81\t(i)Caption-Based Answer Generation.We treat the generated caption ˆCias the sole context to\n82\tanswer the probe questionq k. The predicted answerˆa i,kis generated as:\n83\tˆai,k=M judge(· |Context= ˆCi,Query=q k)(1)\n84\tThis step tests whether the atomic fact queried byq kis recoverable from ˆCi.\n85\t(ii)Semantic Verification.Next, we evaluate the correctness of ˆai,kagainst the ground truth agt\n86\tk.\n87\tThe judge assigns a semantic similarity scores i,k∈[0, S max](whereS max= 5):\n88\tsi,k=Score\u0000\n89\tˆai,k, agt\n90\tk;M judge\u0001\n91\t(2)\n92\t(iii) Dense Reward Aggregation.The raw reward rifor caption ˆCiis the normalized average\n93\tacross the probe setQ, ensuring balanced attention to visual, audio, and joint details:\n94\tri=1\n95\tN·S maxNX\n96\tk=1si,k (3)\n97\tDa-GRPO Optimization Objective.With the atomic detail-aware rewards computed, we employ\n98\tGroup Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to update the policy. Given a video inputx, the policy\n99\tmodelπ θsamples a group of candidate captions{ ˆCi}G\n100\ti=1from the old policyπ θold.\n101\tThe advantage ˆAican be computed as:\n102\tˆAi=ri−mean({r 1, . . . , r G})\n103\tstd({r 1, . . . , r G})(4)\n104\tFinally, the GRPO objective maximizes this advantage subject to a KL-divergence constraint:\n105\tJDa-GRPO (θ) =Ex∼D,{ ˆCi}G\n106\ti=1∼πθold(·|x)\"\n107\t1\n108\tGGX\n109\ti=1 \n110\tπθ(ˆCi|x)\n111\tπθold(ˆCi|x)ˆAi−βD KL(πθ||πref)!#\n112\t(5)\n113\tBy explicitly maximizing this objective, the policy πθis incentivized to generate descriptions that are\n114\tnot only fluent but also semantically detailed and factually aligned with the video content.\n115\t3.3 A VCap-Bench and A VCap-Score\n116\tTo tackle challenges ofModal DisentanglementandAtomic Granularitywhile constructing a\n117\tbenchmark for detailed audio-visual captioning, we establishA VCap-Bench. Its 1,000 examples\n118\tcome from a held-out split fixed before training, and each ground-truth caption is manually checked\n119\tfor fine-grained correspondence with the audiovisual signals.\n120\t5\n121\t--- PAGE 6 ---\n122\t3Human\n123\tChecked\n124\tGround Truth\n125\tGenerated\n126\tCaptionQwen3 -30B -A3B\n127\tAVCap -100K\n128\tTest set...Ground\n129\tTruth\n130\tQ-A Pair\n131\tQwen3 -30B -A3B\n132\t... Predicted\n133\tQ-A PairQ-A TripletQwen3 -30B -A3B\n134\tAVCap -\n135\tScore\n136\t...\n137\tFigure 5:Evaluation Pipeline of A VCap-Score.The metric compares informational density through\n138\ta QA proxy: (1) the top branch derives 20 atomic QA pairs from the human-checked ground-truth\n139\tcaption; (2) the bottom branch answers the same questions using the generated caption as the only\n140\tcontext; and (3) the right branch semantically verifies the predicted answers against the ground truth\n141\tto produce the final score.\n142\tTo quantify performance, we proposeA VCap-Score, an automated metric that measures the informa-\n143\ttional equivalence between the video and the generated caption. Unlike traditional n-gram metrics\n144\t(e.g., CIDEr) that rely on surface-level lexical overlap, A VCap-Score decomposes the evaluation\n145\tinto specific atomic facts, mirroring the granularity of our training objective, following protocols of\n146\tmultimodal post-training works that directly optimize benchmark-aligned core metrics as rewards\n147\t[45–47].\n148\tBenchmark Construction & Pipeline.We construct the test set Dtestby sampling 1,000 videos from\n149\tA VCap-100K. As illustrated in Figure 5, the evaluation proceeds in two branches. Given the human-\n150\tverified caption Cgt, the Judge Model (Qwen3-30B-A3B), denoted as Mjudge, first raises 20 atomic\n151\tquestions qk∼ M judge(Question|Cgt)and derives oracle answers agt\n152\tk=M judge(Answer|q k, Cgt),\n153\tyielding the Gold Standard probe set Q={(q k, agt\n154\tk)}20\n155\tk=1. For a candidate caption ˆC, the same judge\n156\tpredicts ˆak=M judge(Answer|q k,ˆC), and then assigns a semantic similarity score sk∈[0,5] by\n157\tcomparing ˆakagainst agt\n158\tkwith the Judge Model. This score directly measures whether the atomic\n159\tfact queried byq kis recoverable from the generated caption.\n160\tQuestion Generation Principles and Score Calculation.The validity of this pipeline relies on\n161\tthe quality of the probes. We adhere to three principles: the answer must be (1) deterministic, (2)\n162\tinferable from context, and (3) focused on audiovisual granularity. Accordingly, the 20 questions\n163\tcover three complementary categories:Visual Scores ( Qv), which probe object colors, camera\n164\tmovements, OCR text, and spatial relationships;Audio Scores ( Qa), which probe timbre, pitch,\n165\tspecific instruments, background noise, and speaker identity; andJoint Scores ( Qav), which probe\n166\ttemporal synchronization, causality, and source grounding. To ensure interpretability, we normalize\n167\tthe score of each modality to a 0–100 scale. Let sk∈[0,5] denote the semantic similarity score\n168\tassigned by the Judge Model for the k-th question. We define the sub-scores for Visual (Visual-\n169\tScore), Audio (Audio-Score), and Joint (Joint-Score) capabilities as SType=100\n170\t|QType|×5P\n171\tk∈Q Typesk,\n172\twhere Type ∈ {v, a, av} . Finally, the totalA VCap-Scoreis computed as A VCap-Score=P|Q|\n173\tk=1sk,\n174\twithsk∈[0,5] . We additionally audit 100 generated probe–answer pairs to verify that they target\n175\tcaption-grounded fine-grained facts (Appendix B.3).\n176\t4 Experiment\n177\t4.1 Experimental Settings\n178\tImplementation Details.We useQwen3-Omni-30B-Instruct(30B-A3B) as the main backbone\n179\tand train on 32 ×80GiB GPUs across four nodes. Training proceeds in two stages:Stage I\n180\tperforms 1 epoch of full-parameter SFT on 60K videos from A VCap-100K with a global batch size\n181\tof 128, a learning rate of 1e-5, cosine decay, and MoE parallelism (Tensor=2, Expert=4, Pipeline=2,\n182\t6\n183\t--- PAGE 7 ---\n184\tContext=2), taking about 5 hours;Stage IIinitializes from the SFT checkpoint and applies GRPO\n185\tfor 200 steps with LoRA [ 48] (Rank=128, α=256), G= 8 ,N= 20 , a global batch size of 512, a\n186\tlearning rate of 5e-5, β= 0.01 , and vLLM [ 49] for efficient rollout, taking about 23 hours. We\n187\talso trainA VCap-7B-SFTfrom Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and apply Da-GRPO to obtainA VCap-7B; full\n188\thyper-parameters and network topologies are provided in Appendix A.\n189\tTable 1:Performance Comparison on General Audiovisual Caption Benchmarks.We report\n190\tHallucination rates (Hall.) and Missing event rates (Miss) on Video-SALMONN-2 [ 12], performance\n191\ton UGC-VideoCap [ 2], and QA-based caption evaluation scores on DailyOmni [ 3] and WorldSense\n192\t[50].\n193\tModel Size ModalityVideo-SALMONN-2 UGC-VideoCap QA-Based Eval\n194\tMiss↓Hall.↓Total↓Audio↑Visual↑Detail↑Avg.↑DailyOmni↑WorldSense↑\n195\tProprietary Models\n196\tGemini-2.5-Pro - A + V 18.1 13.3 31.3 69.5 74.7 73.7 72.6 60.2 33.8\n197\tGemini-2.5-Flash - A + V 19.3 13.9 33.3 69.1 75.8 74.0 73.0 55.3 31.0\n198\tVisual-Only Baselines\n199\tInternVL3.5 8B V 53.8 25.5 79.4 47.9 64.8 59.5 57.4 – –\n200\tQwen2.5-VL 7B V 40.5 17.0 57.5 46.6 69.1 62.3 59.3 – –\n201\tOpen-Source Audio-Visual Models\n202\tUGC-VideoCaptioner 3B A + V 31.6 17.0 48.6 61.4 58.4 57.5 59.1 17.0 11.2\n203\tHumanOmniV2 7B A + V 49.2 12.3 61.6 45.6 66.3 59.5 57.1 8.2 6.6\n204\tARC-Hunyuan-Video 7B A + V 45.7 12.5 58.2 52.7 56.0 55.8 54.8 8.6 8.7\n205\tQwen2.5-Omni 7B A + V 41.7 15.4 57.1 46.9 66.1 60.0 57.7 13.4 8.6\n206\tMiniCPM-o-2.6 8B A + V 42.2 14.3 56.5 38.6 68.5 57.7 54.9 9.8 7.2\n207\tvideo-SALMONN-2 7B A + V 21.2 17.6 38.8 61.8 71.4 68.5 67.2 29.9 18.2\n208\tA V oCaDO 7B A + V21.116.2 37.3 73.0 74.6 71.8 73.2 50.1 25.7\n209\tQwen3-Omni-Instruct 30B-A3B A + V 32.0 13.6 45.6 67.5 74.8 72.3 71.5 17.5 12.7\n210\tQwen3-Omni-Captioner 30B-A3B A + V 31.0 16.6 47.6 69.0 75.5 72.3 72.5 27.2 14.1\n211\tA VCap-7B-SFT (Ours) 7B A + V 25.5 11.3 36.8 75.6 77.9 72.1 75.2 50.5 30.7\n212\tA VCap-30B (Ours) 30B-A3B A + V 22.4 10.3 32.7 84.1 87.6 83.7 85.1 52.1 34.3\n213\tBenchmarks & Evaluation Protocols.We evaluate our model on representative benchmarks\n214\tthat cover both high-level audiovisual semantics and fine-grained details. First, we use the Video-\n215\tSALMONN-2 testset [ 12], following A V oCaDo [ 1], employing GPT-4.1 as the judge to assess\n216\tgeneral audio-visual understanding. Second, we test on UGCVideoCap [ 2], a benchmark for wild\n217\tuser-generated content, where we follow the official protocol using GPT-4o as the evaluator. We\n218\talso report results on DailyOmni and WorldSense under the QA-based caption evaluation protocol\n219\tintroduced by A V oCaDO [ 1]. Finally, we report results of our proposed A VCap-Score. Following\n220\tHuang et al. [51], Zheng et al. [52], all scores are computed with the open-source Qwen3-30B-A3B-\n221\tInstruct judge to ensure sustainability and reproducibility .\n222\tBaselines.Following the protocol in A V oCaDO [ 1], we benchmark against a comprehensive suite of\n223\tmodels on Video-SALMONN-2 and UGCVideoCap. We include leading open-source A V models\n224\tincluding Qwen2.5-Omni [ 22], A V oCaDO [ 1], HumanOmniV2 [ 53], ARC-Hunyuan-Video [ 54],\n225\tMiniCPM-o-2.6 [ 55], Qwen3-Omni-Instruct and Qwen3-Omni-Captioner. We also include commer-\n226\tcial models Gemini-2.5 series [ 17], and strong vision-only baselines Qwen2.5-VL [ 20], InternVL3.5\n227\t[56] to isolate audio contributions. For DailyOmni and WorldSense, we evaluate under the same\n228\tQA-based protocol as A V oCaDO, while the remaining baseline results are quoted directly from\n229\tA V oCaDO. For our fine-grainedA VCap-Score, we prioritize relevant Omni models and specialized\n230\tcompetitors, specifically Gemini series, Qwen2.5-Omni, HumanOmniV2, UGC-VideoCaptioner [ 2],\n231\tA V oCaDO [ 1], and the Qwen3-Omni family to demonstrate the efficacy and scalable potential of our\n232\ttraining pipeline on larger scale models.\n233\t4.2 Experiment Results\n234\tResults on Existing Audio-Visual Caption Benchmarks.As presented in Table 1, our proposed\n235\tmethod demonstrates consistent superiority across model scales. On Video-SALMONN-2,A VCap-\n236\t7B-SFTmarginally outperforms the previous ope\n237\t--- PAGE 8 ---\n238\tTable 2:Fine-grained Evaluation on A VCap-Score Benchmark.We report the accuracy of atomic\n239\tfact verification across Visual, Audio, and Joint dimensions, where all scores are computed using\n240\tQwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct as the judge.\n241\tModel Size Modality Visual-Score Audio-Score Joint-Score A VCap-Score\n242\tProprietary Models\n243\tGemini-2.5-Pro – A + V 49.70 49.70 51.53 50.62\n244\tGemini-2.5-Flash – A + V 51.11 47.73 51.32 50.37\n245\tOpen-source Baselines\n246\tQwen2.5-Omni 7B A + V 38.19 26.17 36.11 34.14\n247\tHumanOmniV2 7B A + V 36.96 31.74 38.56 36.45\n248\tUGC-VideoCaptioner 3B A + V 38.88 35.31 39.61 38.35\n249\tA V oCaDO 7B A + V 47.48 46.34 45.18 46.05\n250\tQwen3-Omni-Instruct 30B-A3B A + V 48.59 43.49 44.72 45.38\n251\tQwen3-Omni-Captioner 30B-A3B A + V 48.22 45.94 46.70 46.89\n252\tA VCap-7B-SFT (Ours) 7B A + V 47.67 50.62 50.37 49.76\n253\tA VCap-30B (Ours) 30B-A3B A + V 58.10 57.64 56.01 56.94\n254\tthe hallucination rate to10.3, remaining competitive with proprietary systems. On UGC-VideoCap,\n255\tA VCap-7B-SFTsurpasses all baselines, andA VCap-30Breaches an average score of85.1. On\n256\tDailyOmni [ 3] and WorldSense [ 50], A VCap-7B-SFT scores 50.5/30.7, while A VCap-30B reaches\n257\t52.1/34.3, surpassing A V oCaDO and Gemini-2.5-Pro on WorldSense.\n258\t4.3 Ablation Studies of Da-GRPO\n259\tImpact of Probe Question Quantity ( N).We analyze the sensitivity to probe count Nin Figure 6.\n260\tPerformance positively correlates with reward density, yet marginal gains diminish as Napproaches\n261\t20. This saturation aligns with the intrinsic information capacity of our ∼15s video clips, a duration\n262\talso to effectively serve downstream video generation models. While also representing a practical\n263\ttradeoff between resources and reward effectiveness, N= 20 serves as the empirical optimal,\n264\tensuring comprehensive coverage of atomic facts without inducing redundancy.\n265\tImpact of Reward Granularity.To isolate the contribution of our proposed reward mechanism,\n266\twe conduct ablation studies on theA VCap-30Bbackbone, comparingDa-GRPOagainst three\n267\trepresentative baselines ranging from standard supervision to coarse-grained reinforcement learning:\n268\tSFT-Only, which trains the model solely with cross-entropy loss and serves as a lower bound;w/\n269\tN-gram Reward, a traditional approach similar to SCST [ 57] that uses BLEU-4 [ 58] as a rule-based\n270\treward for surface-level lexical overlap; andw/ Holistic Semantic Reward, which directly prompts\n271\tthe Judge Model to assign a single scalar score (1–5) based on the overall semantic similarity between\n272\tthe generated caption and the ground truth. Results in Table 3 separates the SFT and Da-GRPO gains\n273\tacross the dense 7B and MoE 30B-A3B backbones, in Table 4 show that atomic-level reward signals\n274\tconsistently outperform coarse-grained alternatives, confirming the importance ofDa-GRPOfor\n275\tprecise audio-visual grounding.\n276\tTable 3:Controlled Attribution of A VCap-100K and Da-GRPO.We report aggregate scores under\n277\tidentical benchmark protocols.\n278\tModel Backbone A VCap-100K Da-GRPO VS2 Total↓UGC Avg.↑A VCap-Score↑\n279\tQwen2.5-Omni Qwen2.5-Omni-7B× ×57.1 57.7 34.14\n280\tA V oCaDO Qwen2.5-Omni-7B× ×37.3 73.2 46.05\n281\tA VCap-7B-SFT Qwen2.5-Omni-7B ✓ × 36.8 75.2 49.76\n282\tA VCap-7B Qwen2.5-Omni-7B ✓ ✓ 35.9 78.0 51.59\n283\tA VCap-30B-SFT Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B ✓ × 34.3 83.5 53.03\n284\tA VCap-30B Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B ✓ ✓ 32.7 85.1 56.94\n285\t5 Conclusion\n286\tIn this paper, we present a holistic framework for fine-grained audio-visual captioning. First, we\n287\tconstructA VCap-100K, a high-quality dataset that empowers a 7B model to surpass existing baselines.\n288\t8\n289\t--- PAGE 9 ---\n290\tTable 4:Ablation Study of Different Reward Mechanisms.\n291\tMethodVideo-SALMONN-2 UGC-VideoCap A VCap-Score\n292\tMiss↓Hall.↓Total↓Audio↑Visual↑Detail↑Avg.↑Visual↑Audio↑Joint↑Total↑\n293\tA VCap-30B-SFT 23.9 10.4 34.3 82.3 86.2 82.1 83.5 55.72 54.05 51.18 53.03\n294\tw/N-gram Reward 23.5 10.4 33.9 82.2 85.6 80.7 82.8 56.02 53.84 53.64 54.28\n295\tw/Holistic Reward 22.9 11.6 34.5 82.5 85.8 82.6 83.6 56.89 53.92 51.97 53.68\n296\tw/Da-GRPO 22.4 10.3 32.7 84.1 87.6 83.7 85.1 58.10 57.64 56.01 56.94\n297\tFigure 6:Impact of Probe Question Quantity (N).\n298\tSecond, we propose theA VCap Model, which utilizesDa-GRPOto incentivize factually grounded\n299\tdescriptions. Its consistent gains on a dense 7B backbone and a substantially different MoE 30B-A3B\n300\tbackbone demonstrate effectiveness across model scales and architectures. Finally, we establish\n301\tA VCap-BenchandA VCap-Scoreto verify atomic facts across Visual, Audio, and Joint dimensions.\n302\tReferences\n303\t[1]Xinlong Chen, Yue Ding, Weihong Lin, Jingyun Hua, Linli Yao, Yang Shi, Bozhou Li, Yuanxing\n304\tZhang, Qiang Liu, Pengfei Wan, et al. Avocado: An audiovisual video captioner driven by\n305\ttemporal orchestration.arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.10395, 2025.\n306\t[2]Peiran Wu, Yunze Liu, Zhengdong Zhu, Enmin Zhou, and Junxiao Shen. Ugc-videocaptioner:\n307\tAn omni ugc video detail caption model and new benchmarks.arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.11336,\n308\t2025.\n309\t[3]Ziwei Zhou, Rui Wang, and Zuxuan Wu. Daily-omni: Towards audio-visual reasoning with\n310\ttemporal alignment across modalities.arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.17862, 2025.\n311\t[4]Xinlong Chen, Yuanxing Zhang, Chongling Rao, Yushuo Guan, Jiaheng Liu, Fuzheng Zhang,\n312\tChengru Song, Qiang Liu, Di Zhang, and Tieniu Tan. Vidcapbench: A comprehensive\n313\tbenchmark of video captioning for controllable text-to-video generation.arXiv preprint\n314\tarXiv:2502.12782, 2025.\n315\t[5]Lin Chen, Xilin Wei, Jinsong Li, Xiaoyi Dong, Pan Zhang, Yuhang Zang, Zehui Chen, Haodong\n316\tDuan, Zhenyu Tang, Li Yuan, et al. Sharegpt4video: Improving video understanding and\n317\tgeneration with better captions.Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 37:\n318\t1947